WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.579
What is nissology?

00:00:01.579 --> 00:00:07.522
The word was proposed in 1994 by the anthropologist Grant McCall, from the Greek nisos,

00:00:07.522 --> 00:00:08.547
meaning island.

00:00:09.647 --> 00:00:13.311
McCall defined it as the study of islands on their own terms.

00:00:13.311 --> 00:00:15.354
It grew out of a Pacific critique:

00:00:15.354 --> 00:00:20.521
aid agencies and mainland scholars tended to describe islands by what they lack: size,

00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:22.023
resources and connection.

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:28.191
In the same year the Tongan writer Epeli Hau'ofa answered that story of smallness.

00:00:28.191 --> 00:00:33.630
He described Oceania not as scattered specks in an empty ocean, but as a sea of islands,

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.731
connected by voyaging and kinship.

00:00:36.831 --> 00:00:41.209
Island studies then became a field with its own institutions:

00:00:41.209 --> 00:00:46.162
an international association in 1992, Island Studies Journal in 2006,

00:00:46.162 --> 00:00:49.463
and the island cultures journal Shima in 2007.

00:00:50.563 --> 00:00:52.927
Two ideas run through this whole course.

00:00:52.927 --> 00:00:57.359
First, islandness: isolation and boundedness shape what happens on islands,

00:00:57.359 --> 00:00:59.073
but they do not determine it.

00:00:59.073 --> 00:01:01.023
Always ask what the mechanism is.

00:01:01.023 --> 00:01:02.205
Second, whose terms?

00:01:02.205 --> 00:01:05.456
Most research on small islands is written by outsiders.

00:01:05.456 --> 00:01:09.534
That does not make it wrong, but it shapes which questions get asked.

00:01:10.634 --> 00:01:14.691
Your two foundation readings, by Godfrey Baldacchino and Adam Grydehøj,

00:01:14.691 --> 00:01:16.977
are essays about exactly these problems.

00:01:16.977 --> 00:01:20.920
Read them first: they are the lens for every case study that follows.
